1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,913
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reinforcement learning has mastered complex skills,</font>

2
00:00:03,993 --> 00:00:10,625
<font color="#FFFFFF">but almost always by assuming the environment
resets to a fixed initial state after every episode.</font>

3
00:00:10,705 --> 00:00:15,419
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the real world, especially in robotics, those
resets are expensive,</font>

4
00:00:15,499 --> 00:00:18,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">requiring human supervision or scripted routines.</font>

5
00:00:18,855 --> 00:00:23,364
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work, presented at ICML 2023 by Jigang Kim,
Daesol Cho, and H.</font>

6
00:00:23,444 --> 00:00:26,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">Jin Kim from Seoul National University,</font>

7
00:00:26,115 --> 00:00:31,241
<font color="#FFFFFF">tackles autonomous reinforcement learning without
any resets and, crucially,</font>

8
00:00:31,321 --> 00:00:33,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">without any demonstrations.</font>

9
00:00:33,170 --> 00:00:34,802
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their method, called IBC,</font>

10
00:00:34,882 --> 00:00:41,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">combines an auxiliary agent that fades away as the
main agent improves with a bidirectional</font>

11
00:00:41,115 --> 00:00:43,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">goal curriculum built on optimal transport.</font>

12
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:48,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is state-of-the-art performance on
non-episodic benchmarks,</font>

13
00:00:48,854 --> 00:00:55,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">matching methods that rely on expert data and even
approaching episodic oracle reinforcement learning.</font>

14
00:00:56,140 --> 00:01:01,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">Standard reinforcement learning quietly assumes
something that rarely holds outside a</font>

15
00:01:01,515 --> 00:01:04,217
<font color="#FFFFFF">simulator: that at the end of every episode,</font>

16
00:01:04,297 --> 00:01:07,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">the environment magically resets to its starting state.</font>

17
00:01:07,774 --> 00:01:12,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">For a physical robot, resetting means human
intervention, scripted reset policies,</font>

18
00:01:12,959 --> 00:01:15,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">or custom rigs, all of which are slow and costly.</font>

19
00:01:16,057 --> 00:01:21,415
<font color="#FFFFFF">Autonomous reinforcement learning tries to remove
that assumption by learning from one</font>

20
00:01:21,495 --> 00:01:23,944
<font color="#FFFFFF">long uninterrupted stream of experience.</font>

21
00:01:24,024 --> 00:01:29,382
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the existing autonomous methods cheat in a
different way: they rely on prior data,</font>

22
00:01:29,462 --> 00:01:32,986
<font color="#FFFFFF">like expert demonstrations or example states of interest,</font>

23
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:38,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they struggle badly in environments where the
interactions that matter are sparse</font>

24
00:01:38,441 --> 00:01:40,511
<font color="#FFFFFF">and almost never happen by chance.</font>

25
00:01:40,591 --> 00:01:44,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">What has been missing is an agent that learns truly
from scratch,</font>

26
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:46,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no resets and no demonstrations.</font>

27
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:50,841
<font color="#FFFFFF">Why is demonstration-free autonomous learning so hard?</font>

28
00:01:50,921 --> 00:01:52,699
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the non-episodic setting,</font>

29
00:01:52,779 --> 00:01:56,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">an untrained forward agent wanders off to arbitrary
states,</font>

30
00:01:56,692 --> 00:02:02,249
<font color="#FFFFFF">so every new attempt starts from a wildly
different, often useless initial condition.</font>

31
00:02:02,329 --> 00:02:04,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">That instability makes learning collapse.</font>

32
00:02:05,048 --> 00:02:09,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior work patched this in ways that reintroduce
human effort.</font>

33
00:02:09,161 --> 00:02:12,529
<font color="#FFFFFF">Some methods still ask for occasional manual resets.</font>

34
00:02:12,609 --> 00:02:17,504
<font color="#FFFFFF">Others only succeed when the useful interactions
happen to occur by chance.</font>

35
00:02:17,584 --> 00:02:21,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the two most directly comparable methods, VaPRL
and MEDAL,</font>

36
00:02:21,696 --> 00:02:23,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">both lean on demonstration data,</font>

37
00:02:23,818 --> 00:02:29,707
<font color="#FFFFFF">either to seed a subgoal curriculum or to define
what the backward agent should return to.</font>

38
00:02:29,787 --> 00:02:35,875
<font color="#FFFFFF">The motivation here is to build an agent that
provides its own anchor and its own curriculum,</font>

39
00:02:35,955 --> 00:02:38,860
<font color="#FFFFFF">using nothing but the experience it collects.</font>

40
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:43,104
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core contribution is a method called IBC,</font>

41
00:02:43,184 --> 00:02:47,049
<font color="#FFFFFF">short for Implicit and Bidirectional Curriculum.</font>

42
00:02:47,129 --> 00:02:50,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">As far as the authors know,</font>

43
00:02:51,073 --> 00:02:54,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is the first algorithm for non-episodic
reinforcement learning that consistently learns</font>

44
00:02:55,017 --> 00:02:58,881
<font color="#FFFFFF">without any manual resets and without any demonstrations.</font>

45
00:02:58,961 --> 00:03:02,826
<font color="#FFFFFF">It brings together two ideas.</font>

46
00:03:02,906 --> 00:03:06,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">The first is a conditionally activated auxiliary
agent that forms an implicit curriculum:</font>

47
00:03:06,850 --> 00:03:10,714
<font color="#FFFFFF">it helps the main agent early on and then gradually
disappears as the main agent becomes</font>

48
00:03:10,794 --> 00:03:14,659
<font color="#FFFFFF">capable.</font>

49
00:03:14,739 --> 00:03:18,603
<font color="#FFFFFF">The second is a bidirectional goal curriculum,
grounded in optimal transport,</font>

50
00:03:18,683 --> 00:03:22,547
<font color="#FFFFFF">that automatically proposes intermediate goals for
both the forward and the backward directions.</font>

51
00:03:22,627 --> 00:03:26,491
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these let the agent bootstrap its own
training signal,</font>

52
00:03:26,571 --> 00:03:30,436
<font color="#FFFFFF">and across six sparse-reward environments IBC beats
methods that rely on expert data while</font>

53
00:03:30,516 --> 00:03:34,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">approaching the performance of an idealized
episodic oracle.</font>

54
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:36,144
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how IBC works.</font>

55
00:03:36,224 --> 00:03:40,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">During training the agent constantly alternates
between two roles.</font>

56
00:03:40,618 --> 00:03:43,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">The forward agent tries to accomplish the task.</font>

57
00:03:43,746 --> 00:03:50,189
<font color="#FFFFFF">The auxiliary agent brings it back toward a set of
target initial states so it can practice again.</font>

58
00:03:50,269 --> 00:03:56,113
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key trick is that the auxiliary agent is
activated only when the forward agent fails,</font>

59
00:03:56,193 --> 00:03:58,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">so as the forward agent gets better,</font>

60
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:02,037
<font color="#FFFFFF">the auxiliary agent naturally steps in less and less.</font>

61
00:04:02,117 --> 00:04:04,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">That is the implicit curriculum.</font>

62
00:04:04,247 --> 00:04:08,427
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of this sits the explicit, bidirectional
goal curriculum.</font>

63
00:04:08,507 --> 00:04:10,957
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rather than relying on demonstrations,</font>

64
00:04:11,037 --> 00:04:16,814
<font color="#FFFFFF">IBC samples candidate states from its own replay
buffer and frames curriculum generation</font>

65
00:04:16,894 --> 00:04:18,944
<font color="#FFFFFF">as an optimal transport problem,</font>

66
00:04:19,024 --> 00:04:23,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">specifically a Wasserstein Barycenter augmented
with a value bias term.</font>

67
00:04:23,750 --> 00:04:29,395
<font color="#FFFFFF">It solves the resulting bipartite matching with a
Minimum Cost Maximum Flow algorithm,</font>

68
00:04:29,475 --> 00:04:34,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing K intermediate goals for the forward
agent and K for the auxiliary agent.</font>

69
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:41,842
<font color="#FFFFFF">A Lipschitz-continuity assumption lets the method
relax the true objective into a tractable lower bound,</font>

70
00:04:41,922 --> 00:04:45,703
<font color="#FFFFFF">and standard Soft Actor-Critic handles the policy updates.</font>

71
00:04:45,783 --> 00:04:51,028
<font color="#FFFFFF">Remarkably, defining the target distribution needs
only about ten sample states,</font>

72
00:04:51,108 --> 00:04:56,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">and sometimes just one, rather than thousands of
demonstration transitions.</font>

73
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:00,464
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test IBC,</font>

74
00:05:00,544 --> 00:05:04,608
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors assembled six sparse-reward
environments spanning both manipulation and locomotion.</font>

75
00:05:04,688 --> 00:05:08,752
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two of them, Tabletop Manipulation and Sawyer Door,
come from EARL,</font>

76
00:05:08,832 --> 00:05:12,897
<font color="#FFFFFF">an established benchmark for autonomous
reinforcement learning.</font>

77
00:05:12,977 --> 00:05:17,041
<font color="#FFFFFF">The other four are Fetch Pick and Place, Fetch
Push, Fetch Reach, and Point-U-Maze,</font>

78
00:05:17,121 --> 00:05:21,185
<font color="#FFFFFF">which are standard MuJoCo-based OpenAI Gym tasks
that the authors modified for the reset-free,</font>

79
00:05:21,265 --> 00:05:25,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">non-episodic setting.</font>

80
00:05:25,409 --> 00:05:29,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">Evaluation follows the EARL protocol: the agent is
spawned once,</font>

81
00:05:29,553 --> 00:05:33,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">interacts continually, and is reset only rarely,</font>

82
00:05:33,697 --> 00:05:37,762
<font color="#FFFFFF">after hundreds of thousands of steps.</font>

83
00:05:37,842 --> 00:05:41,906
<font color="#FFFFFF">Performance is measured as the deployed policy
evaluation metric,</font>

84
00:05:41,986 --> 00:05:46,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">reported at ten-thousand-step intervals and
averaged across five random seeds.</font>

85
00:05:46,430 --> 00:05:52,611
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is that IBC reaches
state-of-the-art success rates across all six environments,</font>

86
00:05:52,691 --> 00:05:55,521
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it does so without a single demonstration.</font>

87
00:05:55,601 --> 00:05:57,481
<font color="#FFFFFF">It outperforms VaPRL and MEDAL,</font>

88
00:05:57,561 --> 00:06:01,908
<font color="#FFFFFF">two strong baselines that actually use near-expert
demonstration data,</font>

89
00:06:01,988 --> 00:06:07,474
<font color="#FFFFFF">and in many tasks its success rate is comparable to
the oracle: a standard reinforcement</font>

90
00:06:07,554 --> 00:06:09,688
<font color="#FFFFFF">learning agent trained in the easy,</font>

91
00:06:09,768 --> 00:06:11,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">fully-resettable episodic setting.</font>

92
00:06:11,918 --> 00:06:16,771
<font color="#FFFFFF">The demonstration-based baselines especially falter
in the Fetch environments,</font>

93
00:06:16,851 --> 00:06:22,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the task-relevant interactions are sparse and
the evaluation goals are spread across</font>

94
00:06:22,543 --> 00:06:25,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">a whole region rather than a few discrete points.</font>

95
00:06:25,642 --> 00:06:30,052
<font color="#FFFFFF">And when VaPRL is stripped of its demonstrations
for a fair comparison,</font>

96
00:06:30,132 --> 00:06:32,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">its performance drops noticeably,</font>

97
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:36,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">underscoring just how much the prior methods depend
on that extra data.</font>

98
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:41,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablation study peels the method apart to show
that both pieces earn their keep.</font>

99
00:06:41,173 --> 00:06:45,256
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, removing the bidirectional curriculum causes
a consistent drop in performance,</font>

100
00:06:45,336 --> 00:06:49,419
<font color="#FFFFFF">demonstrating the value of gradually guiding the
agent from easy initial states and goals to harder ones.</font>

101
00:06:49,499 --> 00:06:53,582
<font color="#FFFFFF">Then, further removing the auxiliary agent,</font>

102
00:06:53,662 --> 00:06:57,745
<font color="#FFFFFF">which reduces the method to naive reset-free
reinforcement learning,</font>

103
00:06:57,825 --> 00:07:01,908
<font color="#FFFFFF">causes an additional and larger drop, particularly
in the object-manipulation tasks.</font>

104
00:07:01,988 --> 00:07:06,072
<font color="#FFFFFF">The gains are task-dependent: the bidirectional
curriculum matters little in the simple</font>

105
00:07:06,152 --> 00:07:10,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">Tabletop Manipulation environment,</font>

106
00:07:10,315 --> 00:07:14,398
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the auxiliary agent is less helpful in Point-U-Maze,</font>

107
00:07:14,478 --> 00:07:18,561
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the start is already far from the goal.</font>

108
00:07:18,641 --> 00:07:22,724
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also confirm the implicit curriculum
behaves as intended: the fraction of</font>

109
00:07:22,804 --> 00:07:26,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">episodes the auxiliary agent intervenes in falls
toward zero once the forward agent is</font>

110
00:07:26,967 --> 00:07:31,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">fully trained.</font>

111
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:33,603
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

112
00:07:33,683 --> 00:07:36,949
<font color="#FFFFFF">IBC was tested on six sparse-reward environments,</font>

113
00:07:37,029 --> 00:07:41,729
<font color="#FFFFFF">two from the established EARL benchmark and four
adapted MuJoCo tasks,</font>

114
00:07:41,809 --> 00:07:43,914
<font color="#FFFFFF">each run over five random seeds.</font>

115
00:07:43,994 --> 00:07:48,967
<font color="#FFFFFF">To define its target distribution, it needs only
about ten example states,</font>

116
00:07:49,047 --> 00:07:50,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">and sometimes just one,</font>

117
00:07:50,618 --> 00:07:57,025
<font color="#FFFFFF">compared with the thousands of demonstration
transitions that prior autonomous methods require.</font>

118
00:07:57,105 --> 00:08:01,190
<font color="#FFFFFF">And despite using zero demonstrations and zero
manual resets,</font>

119
00:08:01,270 --> 00:08:07,882
<font color="#FFFFFF">it reaches success rates comparable to an oracle
agent trained in the far easier episodic setting.</font>

120
00:08:07,962 --> 00:08:12,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">In short: none of the usual crutches, and roughly
the same performance.</font>

121
00:08:13,110 --> 00:08:15,308
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple to remember.</font>

122
00:08:15,388 --> 00:08:21,558
<font color="#FFFFFF">IBC shows that an agent can learn robotic
manipulation and locomotion tasks entirely on its own,</font>

123
00:08:21,638 --> 00:08:26,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no environment resets and no demonstrations,
by generating its own curriculum.</font>

124
00:08:27,041 --> 00:08:34,186
<font color="#FFFFFF">It does this with two cooperating ideas: an
auxiliary agent that anchors the learner early and then fades away,</font>

125
00:08:34,266 --> 00:08:41,607
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a bidirectional goal curriculum built on
optimal transport that keeps proposing achievable intermediate goals.</font>

126
00:08:41,687 --> 00:08:45,123
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result matches methods that depend on expert data.</font>

127
00:08:45,203 --> 00:08:52,413
<font color="#FFFFFF">The main caveats are that it assumes reversible
environments and still needs a human to specify a sparse reward,</font>

128
00:08:52,493 --> 00:08:55,863
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the authors point to a fully reward-free version,</font>

129
00:08:55,943 --> 00:09:00,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">hinted at by their C-learning experiments, as the
natural next step.</font>
